WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
- [Narrator] Two-and-a-half
hours outside of Seattle,

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.456
in this sleepy little
town of Yakima, Washington,

00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:06.916
lies a hidden empire.

00:00:06.916 --> 00:00:08.730
And what they've been making

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.240
you've used your entire life.
- It's like

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.632
one of those things
that you go, “Oh my God!”

00:00:13.632 --> 00:00:16.060
“That was invented?

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.017
“Oh, yeah, wow!”

00:00:22.163 --> 00:00:24.840
My name is Stephanie, and I'm the oldest

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.316
of the Paxton women.

00:00:26.316 --> 00:00:30.420
- My name is Kimberly and
I'm in the middle. (laughing)

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.693
- My name is Melissa, and I am the baby.

00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:36.366
We are the three sisters

00:00:36.366 --> 00:00:38.930
behind the Kwik Lok corporation

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:40.960
and the little bag closures that you see

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.677
just about everywhere.

00:00:42.677 --> 00:00:45.243
- [Narrator] And by everywhere,

00:00:45.243 --> 00:00:47.517
she means everywhere.

00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:48.768
- Typically around bread,

00:00:48.768 --> 00:00:52.289
but it can be found on
a variety of packages

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:56.300
from produce to potatoes.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.232
We sell billions of Kwik
Loks a year, billlllions.

00:00:59.232 --> 00:01:03.180
(group laughing)

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.780
- [Narrator] The invention of the little

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.530
closures that could dates back

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.810
to their Grandfather, Floyd Paxton.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.430
- The story certainly is

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.860
that our Grandfather
whittled his first Kwik Lok

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:13.960
out of a credit card.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.740
Whether or not that is actual fact

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.252
will probably never be discovered.

00:01:18.252 --> 00:01:21.045
- [Narrator] But the bag closure business

00:01:21.045 --> 00:01:22.413
isn't all fun and games.

00:01:22.413 --> 00:01:23.510
- I would say our biggest competition

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:24.790
is the wire tie.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.422
You know the thing that you twist,

00:01:27.422 --> 00:01:30.499
that you can't untwist
or get it all mixed up.

00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:32.460
- [Narrator] Oh, yeah, those things.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.110
Never thought about it, but if we're

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.260
being honest, I guess
I never really thought

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.652
about bag closures in general.

00:01:38.652 --> 00:01:42.974
- It's always so fun telling
the story of the Kwik Lok.

00:01:42.974 --> 00:01:45.611
It’s this little plastic square closure

00:01:45.611 --> 00:01:48.685
that was invented in Yakima, Washington,

00:01:48.685 --> 00:01:51.300
and has gone from this little town

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.153
to being sold globally
and internationally.

